## Item text
Best Outdoor and Camping Blankets of 2026

Puffy Blankets, Picnic Blankets, Waterproof Blankets, and Fleece and Down Blankets for Camping, Travel, Festivals, and Cabins

October 1st, 2026, updated with four new winnersHome >Gear Reviews >Camping

Camping blankets are not just about staying cozy. They are built for the dirt, mud, spills, and damp conditions that come with spending time outside. Unlike the old sheet or bed comforter many of us used to haul along, a good outdoor blanket should be easy to clean, resistant to moisture, and compact enough that you will actually bring it.

I first encountered puffy outdoor blankets at a trade show in 2016 and have been using and evaluating them ever since. For this update, I took them on 40 flights, desert trips in Joshua Tree and the Mojave, a month-long summer camp in the Eastern Sierra with other testers, five-day fishing trips in Montana, and outdoor festivals in Oregon. We compared water resistance by suspending each blanket like a hammock, pouring half a liter of water onto it, and timing how long it took to leak through. Some held the water for more than six hours without fully wetting through.

We evaluated each blanket for warmth, water resistance, weight, packed size, durability, comfort, and ease of cleaning. We also considered whether it worked best as an insulating layer, a ground cover, or a blanket that could reasonably do both.

Top Pick: The Rumpl Original Puffy Blanket earns our Best Overall award. It stood out for its water resistance, durable recycled construction, easy machine washability, and useful balance of warmth and packability. In our spill test, it did not begin leaking through the stitching for 15 minutes and was still holding most of the water three hours later. Corner loops make it easier to secure in windy conditions, while its range of sizes and designs works for solo camping, festivals, road trips, and everyday outdoor use.

Note: This guide includes insulated blankets designed primarily for warmth, durable ground blankets for picnics and damp campsites, and versatile options that can handle both jobs.

We create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.

Comparison table

The Winners

Best Outdoor Blanket: Rumpl Original Puffy Blanket

Best uses: Camping, picnics, festivalsSizes available: 28" x 40" (mini) / 38" x 52" (compact throw) / 52" x 75" (1-person/full) / 84" x 80" (2-person)Insulation: 100% recycled 3D hollow fiber siliconized synthetic insulation; 240 gsmShell material: 100% recycled 30D ripstop polyester fabricPackable: YesWater-resistant: YesWashing: Machine washableWhat we liked: refined stitching and quality, fun designs and colors, variety of sizes, eco-friendly company, corner loopsWhat we didn't like: price, face material is shiny

The Rumpl Original Puffy Blanket is what you think of when you think of a puffy blanket for outdoor use. It's soft, comes in beautiful designs, is well-stitched, and comes with a convenient tote bag that makes it pack down to a small size. It works well to break the chill around a campfire (or on the couch!). It's a shareable alternative to a sleeping bag on warm days and adds extra warmth on top of a sleeping bag on cold nights. We even used it as a picnic blanket at festivals because it's so packable.

Best yet, Rumpl is one of the most eco-friendly outdoor brands and is a Climate Neutral Certified brand where 100% of the shell and insulation are post-consumer recycled materials and 100 plastic bottles are recycled for each double blanket.

There are still more color and print options with Rumpl than other brands, so we still think it's the best camping blanket.

The Rumpl Original comes in beautiful designs that support artists, like this blanket by Tlingit and Athabascan artist Crystal Worl. Photo by Kate Hoch.

The Rumpl Original Puffy blanket can be used instead of a sleeping bag on warm nights. Photo by Kate Hoch.

Updates

New for this year, the Rumpl Original is available in mini and throw sizes designed for kids or pets.

Sizes

The Rumpl Original Puffy blanket is available in three sizes—a junior, a single (75x52 inches) and a double (84×80 inches). In our experience, the single can easily be shared with two people when using it as a picnic blanket. If the couple is sharing a double camp chair like the Kelty Low, the single should be fine, too. But if you're looking to use the blanket at night instead of a sleeping bag, you'll want the double, no matter how well you know the other person!

Patterns and colors

The biggest advantage of the Rumpl over other blankets is that it has the most options, colors, patterns, and designs. Camping blankets are large and many people make a statement with them. From the RAD (Rumpl Artist Division) that supports outdoors artists to their National Park line, there are colors, patterns, and designs for everyone. We tested a blanket with a design by Crystal Worl, a Tlingit and Athabascan artist.

Cape clip

Another less obvious perk of the Rumpl blanket over other puffy blankets is the Cape Clip, which allows you to turn it into a cape for walking around camp. You can even use it as a changing poncho in a pinch, though we used it for a month at the camp's outdoor shower and it required getting creative.

The Rumpl is one of the few blankets we tested that has corner loops to stake out your blanket on windy days.

Warmth

The Rumpl Original sits in the middle of the pack in terms of warmth. It isn't the warmest blanket, but it's noticeably warmer than others we tested, including the similar looking Cascade Mountain Tech. We used it as an alternative to a sleeping bag while camping by hot springs in the Eastern Sierra in the peak of summer and didn't find it warm enough for a night in the 40s. But we found it perfect for sitting around a campfire, sitting on the porch of a cabin for sunset, or watching an outdoor movie.

Water-resistance

The Rumpl Original is also among the more water-resistant camp blankets we tested. When we dumped a 0.5 L Nalgene bottle on it, it took 15 minutes to start to leak through, and that was only at the stitching. We cut the experiment off after 3 hours, but even then, it was still holding most of the water. Since it has synthetic insulation, the Rumpl stays warm, even when it's misty or wet, unlike down blankets.

Easy washing

The Original Puffy Blanket is made of easy-to-wash synthetic insulation that can be thrown in the washing machine, which is handy since we always used our blanket around the campfire and wanted to remove the smoke odor.

For its balance of warmth, price, sustainability, ease of washing, packability, and cool patterns and designs, the Rumpl Original is an easy overall winner for best camping blanket.

Best Picnic Blanket: Nomadix Festival Blanket

Best uses: Picnics, camping, lawn sitting, festivalsSizes available: 72" x 60"Insulation: n/aShell material: 88% post-consumer recycled polyesterPackable: YesWater-resistant: YesWashing: YesWhat we liked: waterproofness, dimensions, grippiness, repels sand, recycled material, designs, corner loopsWhat we didn't like: price, does not come with stuff sack or stuffable pocket

The Nomadix Festival Blanket is our winner for best picnic blanket. It's so waterproof, it never wetted out or leaked in over 3 hours of testing supporting half a liter of water. It’s made of a durable material that doesn’t stretch or warp over time and washes. It's grippy enough to use as a hot yoga mat. It has corner loops so it doesn't blow away. I had always thought an old sheet was sufficient enough for a picnic blanket until testing the Festival Blanket, and now that I've seen how good things can be, I never want to go back.

We like that the Nomadix packs down small. Like the Rumpl Everywhere Mat, it also has loops on the corner to prevent it from blowing away in the wind. Our one wish for it is that it had a stuff sack or folded into its own pocket. Still, the size, waterproofing, eco-friendly material, and price all make it a winner.

The Nomadix Festival Blanket makes a great porch for your camping tent. Photo by Kate Hoch.

Size comparison between the Festival Blanket and Everywhere Mat. Photo by Kate Hoch.

Corner loop to stake down your blanket and prevent it from blowing away in the wind. Photo by Kate Hoch.

Slip-resistant

The Nomadix is slip resistant, which it claims makes it good for hot yoga. To test this claim, we took it to our studio. While this blanket isn’t a typical hot yoga mat or hot yoga towel and I felt a little weird doing it, the Nomadix blanket stayed grippy even when wet. That grippiness also proves useful outdoors, such as when you're using it as a beach blanket if your feet are still wet coming out of the water.

Stain-resistant

The backside of this blanket is black, so it doesn’t show stains where it is against the ground. In our testing, sand and dirt didn't stick as much to the bottom material as the top material. The top side (where others will see it) comes in fun prints. We wish there were more colors, though.

Sustainability

The Nomadix Festival Blanket is made of Global Recycled Standard-certified post-consumer recycled plastic. The Global Recycled Standard (GRS) considered environmental impacts as well as social impacts like conditions for workers.

Comparison to other picnic blankets

The Festival Blanket and Rumpl Everywhere Mat (review below) have many of the same features including corner loops. Main differences we found were in size, weight (32 oz vs 19 oz) and price ($69 vs $80). The Festival Blanket is less expensive and bigger, which is why we recommend it. However, it does not stuff into its own pocket, a feature you can find in the Everywhere Mat.

We liked the Festival Blanket's size better for gatherings with more than three people. We liked the Festival Blanket better for using as a porch with 6-person tents.

My biggest issue with the Festival Blanket is I wish it had more color and design options (admittedly, the Rumpl Blanket has about the same offerings). The price is steep as well. Still, its waterproofness is impressive and I have a hard time imagining buying this for myself, but it's the sort of item I would be thrilled to receive as a gift.

Warmest Outdoor Blanket: Rumpl Backcountry Blanket

Best uses: Camping, backpacking, travelSizes available: 74 x 52" (1-person) / 84 x 80" (2-person)Insulation: SyntheticShell material: 100% post consumer recycled shellPackable: YesWater-resistant: YesWashing: YesWhat we liked: Very warm for its weight and packability, balances price, weather-proofness, machine-washability, and warmthWhat we didn't like: warmer options exist made of down (but they are not as affordable or as easy to wash)

The Rumpl Backcountry Blanket is Rumpl's newest blanket and the warmest in their line-up. After trying dozens of blankets that aren't warm enough, we finally found one that keeps off the chill. The only other blankets that were warmer were nearly double the price and harder to wash because they were made out of down. We think if you want a warm blanket, the Backcountry Blanket is the warmest that balances price, packability, weather-proofness, and machine-washability.

Rumpl says it is 20% warmer and 25% more packable than the Original Puffy Blanket and while we can't discern down to the percentage point, we agree with the sentiment.

We also liked the color and pattern options on the Backcountry Blanket, which often aren't available with the heavier-duty camp blankets. The Backcountry blanket also comes in one of the easiest-to-use stuff sacks with a secure buckle. Plus, Rumpl is one of the most sustainable and ethical brands.

Warmth

While the Western Mountaineering Cloud 9 Comforter (below) technically is warmer, we think most campers and travelers will find the Backcountry Blanket to be a much more affordable and easier to use option. We’ve used it on ridges in freezing temperatures with 35 mph winds in snow flurries and light rain. It kept us warm and the weatherproofing kept us dry as we hiked in these conditions while wearing it, using the Cape Clip over our backpack to keep it in place.

The Backcountry Puffy is designed to be more packable and weather-resistant than Rumpl's previous models of warm blankets, including their (now discontinued) Down Blanket.

Waterproofing

In our waterproof testing, the Rumpl Backcountry kept 0.5 L of water from coming through for more than 6 hours (we stopped the test before it ever broke through).

Cape clip to wear it

As with other Rumpl Blankets, the Backcountry Puffy comes with the Cape Clip to make it wearable as well as Corner Staking loops in case you want to turn it into a picnic blanket.

Excellent dry bag included

We also liked that the stuff sack is a roll-top dry bag-style, which is much easier to stuff than the traditional stuff sack found on the Rumpl Original blanket. You could easily attach it to a backpack with a carabiner or lashing if you wanted extra warmth. It also has a zip pocket in the corner so you don’t lose the stuff sack when in motion.

Sustainability

The Backcountry Blanket is made of post-consumer recycled content and the brand is a B-Corp that is Certified Climate Neutral and also a member of 1% for the Planet.

Best wearable outdoor blanket: Therm-a-Rest Honcho Poncho

Best uses: Camping, sleep system layeringSizes available: 79" x 56" (synthetic) / 48" x 42" (down)Insulation: 85% recycled polyester hollow fiber / RDS-certified 650-Fill Nikwax Hydrophobic DownShell material: PFAS-free DWR-coated and 100% Recycled 20D Nylon RipstopPackable: YesWater-resistant: YesWashing: Synthetic version is easier to wash, Down version requires special washing instructions What we liked: Extremely warm, very packable, stylish, fits in its own pocketWhat we didn't like: Price, difficulty of washing down

The Therm-a-Rest Honcho Poncho is the best wearable outdoor blanket. While other outdoor blankets come with hooks or clips to turn it into a cape or hands-free blanket, the Honcho Poncho leans into being wearable above all else.

Now available with more affordable and water-resistant synthetic fill or as a down poncho, the Honcho Poncho is one of the most fought over items around our campfire.

The synthetic version opens into a rectangular blanket, while the down version doesn’t. But you may not want to take either version off.

Yes, the Honcho Poncho has a select use case scenario, but we love this blanket so much, we strongly considered it as the best overall camp blanket.

The author wearing the Honcho Poncho at the outdoor deck at the San Francisco airport during a long layover.

Camping with the Honcho Poncho is easy because it fits into its own small size pouch.

Updates with a down and synthetic version

The Therm-a-Rest Honcho Poncho got an update with two options available: a synthetic and a down version. The synthetic version is more affordable and turns into a blanket.

Waterproofing

Both the down and synthetic versions use a PFAS-free DWR waterproofing on the recycled nylon shell material. The Honcho Poncho impressed us with its ability to keep 0.5 L of water from pouring through and wetting out the down insulation over more than three hours.

The synthetic version is made with a hollow-fiber synthetic insulation. As with other synthetic insulation, even if the shell material were to wet out, it would still maintain much of its insulating properties, even if it were to get wet. See Which is better: down vs. synthetic? for more information.

Material

It’s made of soft down and silky sleeping bag material that is more delicate than the nylon shell on other blankets, but feels better against the skin. The design of the poncho itself is thoughtful—three clip buttons on the side to reduce the flap and a generous half zipper around the neck and chest for ventilation. It also has a hood, so you don’t have to run back to get your hat.

Warmth

I’ve used this on cold nights around the campfire. I’ve also brought it in my carry-on while traveling and it was a lifesaver while working from an outdoor patio at the San Francisco airport during a cold and foggy layover in August (if you know, you know).

Style

It’s even kind of fashionable. At a backcountry “fancy” party in the Eastern Sierra, I won the unofficial prize for best dressed while wearing the Therm-a-Rest Honcho Poncho as a puffy dress with a festive belt (beating out a guy who picked up a 3-piece baby blue suit from the June Lake thrift store!).

Versatility

The best part is that the Honcho Poncho fits into its own kangaroo pocket, so it can be stashed away in a carry-on or backpack. Packed in its pocket, it can even be used as a pillow. The downside is this blanket is expensive, but with quality sleeping bag-grade materials, I expect this poncho to last as long as a down sleeping bag, which means 10 years or more.

Best for Cold Weather: Western Mountaineering Cloud 9 Comforter

Best uses: Camping, travelSizes available: 87" x 55"(twin) / 92" x 87" (queen) / 102" x 92" (king)Insulation: 800-fill European goose downShell material: Nylon Packable: YesWater-resistant: YesWashing: Requires special washing instructionsWhat we liked: Warmth, softness, waterproofness, comfort, high-quality materialsWhat we didn't like: Price, corner ties, lack of color options

For campers and backpackers who want the warmth of a sleeping bag without the restrictiveness (or awkwardness) of a mummy bag, the made in the USA Western Mountaineering Cloud 9 is the best camp blanket for cold weather.

The Western Mountaineering Cloud 9 is a luxurious and thick puffy blanket that packs down to the size of a Nalgene bottle. Its rectangular shape is available in twin, queen, and king sizes–to reflect that it is trying to be the anti-mummy bag–and is completely non-constricting just like your bed at home.

I've been using my Cloud 9 comforter on my bed at home for almost a decade because I like how fluffy and comfortable it feels. It's also a great addition to bring on cold plane and train trips because it is so compact.

The Western Mountaineering Cloud 9 Comforter can be used for extra warmth in addition to a sleeping bag while camping. Photo by Kate Hoch.

The Western Mountaineering Cloud 9 Comforter (right) has a rectangular blanket shape for people who don't enjoy the narrowness of sleeping bags (on the left, for comparison). Photo by Jill Duncan.

Drying out the Western Mountaineering Cloud 9 Comforter on the porch of a cabin in the Sierra. You can see how much loft it has in this photo.

The Western Mountaineering Cloud 9 Comforter is the warmest camping puffy blanket we tested.

Insulation

The Western Mountaineering Cloud 9 Comforter is stuffed with 800-fill European goose down, which is the same insulation found in Western Mountaineering's high-end sleeping bags. Each square sewn through baffle is as puffy as a cloud. It's a joy to snuggle with and does its job of being an extremely effective insulator. You can read about down fill and how down works for insulation in our guide to Best Down Jackets or Best Sleeping Bags.

Warmth

The Western Mountaineering Cloud 9 Comforter is designed to be an alternative to a down sleeping bag. It is made of the same high-end materials you'd find in a Western Mountaineering down sleeping bag, but with a rectangular shape. This makes it excellent as a camping sleeping bag or as an alternative to a backpacking quilt.

While camping in Oregon, I found its size and shape worked particularly well with a camping cot. It was much easier to work around the edges compared to the sleeping bag I also brought (and subsequently pushed to the side).

Straps

The Western Mountaineering Cloud Comfort has straps on each corner so you can tie it closer to your body or secure it to your sleeping pad or even to use in a camping hammock. However, this attachment system relies on your ability to tie knots. In our testing for our backpacking quilts guide, we've found far more sophisticated and easy-to-use systems. While we appreciate the option of the tie-outs and they're better than nothing, we find them too analog to want to use except in the colder conditions.

Washing

Like all down blankets, the Western Mountaineering Cloud 9 is not the easiest to wash. It's also quite expensive. But if you're looking for a down comforter for camping that you can also use for home, it's the best blanket.

Our verdict

If you're looking for a warm and compact blanket to use for travel, we also think the Western Mountaineering is the best option. It packs down to a 6"x10" size stuff sack (4.5L). It isn't the lightest blanket at 25 oz, but it is not even in the same league of warmth as the lighter-weight blankets we tested. 25 oz is excellent for a down sleeping bag or a backpacking quilt and that is the level of warmth you'll get with this bag.

Best wool camp blanket: Sackcloth and Ashes Camp Blanket

Best uses: Cabins, indoor-outdoor useSizes available: 72 x 60"Insulation: n/aShell material: 50% acrylic /25% polyester /20% wool /5% other fabricPackable: Yes, rolls into stylish leather clothWater-resistant: Yes on grass, no when pouring a water bottle on itWashing: Machine-washableWhat we liked: Incredibly stylish, beautiful art, eco-friendly, ethical company donates to homeless shelters, versatility at home and outdoors, affordable for a wool blanket, machine-washableWhat we didn't like: Not as packable or water-resistant as other blankets

The Sackcloth and Ashes outdoor blanket receives rave reviews and we decided to test it based on a Treeline reader's recommendation. This is a beautiful blanket that you'd expect to see on some outdoor Instagram influencer's account as they sit atop a mountain looking at sunset.

But it turns out, looking good doesn't mean something isn't functional. After almost 3 years of testing the Sackcloth and Ashes, we found even more to love about it even beyond its attractive style.

It's the softest blanket we tested. The Sackcloth and Ashes is warm, it's water resistant, it's eco-friendly and the brand donates to shelters for people experiencing houselessness. And unlike most wool blankets, it's not itchy and is actually washable.

So yes, the Sackcloth and Ashes blanket shows it is possible to look good and be a functional outdoor blanket, too.

Style

There's no ignoring the Sackcloth and Ashes outdoor blanket's style. For context, you can get this blanket at Saks Fifth Avenue. Still, the price is reasonable, especially considering that it is made in Italy and finished in the US and a blanket is donated to a homeless shelter for every one you purchase.

Ethical and sustainable

As mentioned above, Sackcloth and Ashes donates a blanket to a homeless shelter for every blanket that is purchased. The material itself is made of recycled acrylic and recycled polyester. The wool is made from remnants from other factory floors to divert it from the landfill. The brand says each blanket is made of 75-100% recycled materials.

The brand says their blankets are "intentionally and responsibly made," and this seems to be one of the better examples I've seen in the outdoor industry.

Machine-washable materials

Ok, this is where I'm putting a "stretch" to the award of Best Wool Blanket. This blanket has only 20% wool. Normally, I would ding myself for that error, but this blanket looks like a traditional wool blanket. And by not having more wool, it means you can actually wash it in a washing machine. It also isn't itchy like wool.

Our verdict

For the beauty of patterns (including designs by indigenous artists), eco-friendly materials, and ethical donation of blankets–as well as the functionality of this blanket, we think it's worth getting if you want something that looks and feels luxurious that you can feel good about purchasing.

Best packable camp blanket: Matador Pocket Blanket

Best uses: Travel, festivals, picnics, hiking Sizes available: 63" x 44"Insulation: n/aShell material: 20D recycled nylon, 70D Bluesign span, 70D upcycled polyester (everything PFAS-free)Packable: YesWater-resistant: YesWashing: Machine-washableWhat we liked: Fits in its own pocket, small enough to fit in your pocket, lightweight, folding directions printed right on the blanket, water-resistant, eco-friendly materialsWhat we didn't like: small size, minimal padding isn’t as luxurious-feeling as padded blankets

The best packable blanket is the Matador Pocket Blanket. At 3.8 oz and packing down to the size of (you guessed it) a pocket, this blanket was designed for on-the-go travel.

The Matador Pocket Blanket was originally designed for staying dry while sitting on wet grass at music festivals. It folds into its own attached storage pouch that can easily fit in a jacket pocket. When you get to where you want to sit, it unfolds to 63" x 44", which can seat 2-4 adults depending on how comfortable you are with each other.

Attached storage pouch

The Matador Pocket Blanket comes with a sewn-in pouch. The entire blanket fits inside. The pouch also has a loop so you can attach the whole system to a pack via carabiner.

Folds back into storage pouch

The Matador Pocket Blanket is the only blanket we've seen that has the folding lines and order of the folds directly printed on the blanket. This means you never have to guess how to get the blanket to fit back into the pocket.

How we tested

We tested at music festivals, on numerous hiking trips in the Sierra, and on urban hikes across Los Angeles. I wasn’t expecting it to be so handy on an urban hike in a dry climate, but the Matador Pocket blanket was a game changer. The only places to take breaks were lawns in front of elementary schools. The sprinklers had just gone off and the Matador blanket kept us all dry while we rested our cement-pounded legs.

Best waterproof outdoor blanket: Rumpl Stash Mat

Best uses: Festivals, picnics, campingShell material: 100% recycled VersaWeave™ 30D ripstop polyester fabricPackable: YesWater-resistant: YesWashing: Spot-clean; hang dryWhat we liked: Waterproof on the bottom, soft on the top, folds into easy carrying case with pockets, includes shoulder-strap so you can use your hands to carry other stuffWhat we didn't like: Outer pockets don't zip

The Rumpl Stash Mat is the best waterproof camp blanket.

The Rumpl Stash mat is two-sided with a soft, puffy blanket on top and extremely waterproof ground mat on the bottom. It's soft (but not slippery) on top but rugged and waterproof on the bottom.

The Rumpl Stash mat is extremely packable, fitting into its own zip pouch that has a shoulder sling.

We are obsessed with the Rumpl Stash blanket and bring it nearly everywhere with us. Simple, practical, multi-use, and easy to grab-and-go, the Rumpl Stash nearly took our best overall blanket award. Not only have I told all my friends about it, the Rumpl Stash will be winning this author's nomination for Gear of the Year.

How we tested

New for this year, we spent the past 6 months using this camp blanket from wet and foggy Redwoods National Park to Big Sur beaches and next to alpine lakes. We used the Rumpl Stash Mat on wet grass at parks and music festivals.

Zips into its own integrated carrying case

The whole blanket easily folds into itself and zips into an integrated carrying case. Every time I pack it down, it feels like magic. We like blankets with integrated stuff sacks but often they mean a sewn-in pocket hanging off the edge of the blanket. Not the end of the world, but not quite aesthetically-pleasing. With the Stash Blanket, the whole thing zips into a pocket, which means there isn't a weird pouch sticking off the side.

Shoulder Strap

It comes with a shoulder strap so you can carry this blanket without using your hands.

Even more pockets

On the outside of the case are two more open pockets to store things like towels, sunscreen, or drinks. The outer pocket doesn't zip, but it has a paracord key loop so you don't have to worry about your key falling out.

Materials

The bottom of the Rumpl Stash is a 250D polyester outer shell designed to shake off moisture, dirt and debris. This is a super rugged 100% postconsumer recycled polyester oxford canvas bottom layer. Aside from maybe our winner for best picnic blanket, this is the most tough-feeling, waterproof material we tested for a camp blanket. It's not soft, but you won't be sitting on the bottom part, so it's ok.

The top part is a 100% postconsumer recycled insulated padding that feels as soft as the Original Rumpl blanket. It's not slippery. You can't feel pine needles poking through it. It's just a great blanket with an even more durable bottom.

Both the top and bottom PFAS-free Durable Water Repellent (DWR).

Best Emergency Camp Blanket: SOL Emergency Blanket

Best uses: BackpackingShell material: PlasticPackable: YesWater-resistant: YesWashing: Cannot washWhat we liked: Warmth for weight, packability, bright orange increases visibilityWhat we didn't like: Emergency blankets as a genre aren't comfortable, this is a more expensive emergency blanket option

The SOL Emergency Blanket is the best outdoor blanket to keep around for emergencies. It’s small, lightweight, and compact, and is designed for situations where you really don’t want to carry a sleeping bag but should probably have a source of warmth, just in case.

While most emergency blankets are very similar, one distinction that makes the SOL Emergency Blanket stand out is that one side is bright orange, making you more visible in case you need to be found.

Warmth

I tested the warmth capabilities of the SOL Emergency Blanket on an ill-fated alpine climb in Yosemite’s high country where we summitted at sunset and then had to rappel slowly down in the dark. It was surprisingly warm given its weight and thin material. Like all emergency blankets, the SOL uses vacuum-metalized polyethylene and is not very durable.

What makes it better

All emergency blankets are fairly similar and the technology hasn't changed significantly since the early days. One advantage of the SOL over other blankets are its dimensions and that it is made of recycled material.

Still, I carry one with me on almost all my winter day hikes in snowy conditions because I have seen how much they really do work when you need it.

We also liked how the SOL emergency blanket is available in an XL size as well as in 4-packs. This just makes emergency blankets more useful for bigger bodies and for families.

Other Outdoor Blankets We Tested

Rumpl Everywhere Mat

